{
    "verdict": "reject",
    "blockers": [
        {
            "claim": "Removing qttune_trusted_count violates the documented vendor bug workaround. The code comment explicitly states that some vendors leave pNumMsgs untouched when returning an error (e.g., 'OP_WRITE: status 7, written 1'), meaning a nonzero count under a hard error is not evidence any message was written. The helper function was the correct defense against this bug.",
            "file": "core/include/qttune/j2534_ptypes.h",
            "quote": "/* Finding 1 (code review 2026-10-20): trust rule for vendor-reported\n * message counts.\n *\n * Some vendors leave pNumMsgs (and the caller's message buffer)\n * untouched when returning an error, so a nonzero count under a hard\n * error is not evidence any message was written. Observed live on the\n * OBDX Pro VX quiet bus: \"OP_WRITE: status 7, written 1\"\n * (bench-sessions/2026-10-20.md). Only these statuses carry a count\n * the caller may trust:\n *\n *   STATUS_NOERROR       - all requested messages read\n *   ERR_TIMEOUT          - partial batch already copied (J2534-1)\n *   ERR_BUFFER_OVERFLOW  - pNumMsgs = messages actually copied\n *\n * Every other status (ERR_FAILED, ERR_BUFFER_EMPTY, device errors,\n * ...) means \"assume nothing was written\". Applies to ReadMsgs; the\n * write-side count is only ever logged, never consumed, and the raw\n * pair is deliberately preserved in logs (it is diagnostic evidence\n * of exactly this vendor behavior). */"
        },
        {
            "claim": "The j2534_loader.cpp removal of qttune_trusted_count creates an unsafe code path where the num variable is used directly without validation. The comment in the removed surrogate code warns: 'A dirty count is doubly dangerous here: s_read_batch is static and never cleared, so it would replay the PREVIOUS read's frames as phantoms.' This same risk exists in the loader.",
            "file": "core/src/j2534_loader.cpp",
            "quote": "num = qttune_trusted_count(rc, num);   /* finding 1: dirty count\n                                               under a hard error is\n                                               not a delivered frame */"
        }
    ],
    "questions": [
        "What is the expected behavior of the loader when QTTUNE_PT_STATUS_TIMEOUT (0x09L) is returned with a nonzero count? The proposal keeps trusting the count for this status, but the comment says it's only for STATUS_NOERROR, ERR_TIMEOUT, and ERR_BUFFER_OVERFLOW. The diff shows STATUS_TIMEOUT is trusted in the old code.",
        "How does this change interact with the PASSTHRU_MSG buffer size assumptions? The struct has Data[QTTUNE_PT_MSG_DATA_SIZE] but the proposal doesn't address whether this is 8-byte CAN or 64+ byte CAN-FD."
    ],
    "nits": [
        "The surrogate code comment mentions 's_read_batch is static and never cleared' which suggests a potential memory safety issue that should be addressed separately.",
        "Consider adding a code comment explaining why the trust rule was deemed unnecessary for this specific use case, if the reviewers disagree with the original finding."
    ]
}
